---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import LatencyComparison from '../../components/LatencyComparison.jsx';
---

<ToolLayout
  title="AI Provider Latency Comparison"
  description="Check current response latency to OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google AI endpoints — useful for diagnosing slow API responses."
>
  <LatencyComparison client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why latency varies</h2>
  <p>
    API response time depends on network routing, provider load, and your
    server's geographic distance from the provider's infrastructure. This
    tool gives you a snapshot from our server, which is useful for spotting
    if one provider is unusually slow right now.
  </p>

  <h2>AI Provider Latency FAQ</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Is this the latency I'll actually see?</strong> Not exactly —
    this measures latency from our server's location, not yours. Use it as
    a relative comparison between providers, not an absolute number for your
    setup.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>How often is the data refreshed?</strong> Each page load takes a
    fresh live measurement — there's no cached or historical data, so
    reloading gives you a new real-time snapshot.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Why do providers sometimes swap rankings between checks?</strong>
    Latency depends on real-time network conditions and provider load, both
    of which fluctuate — a provider that's fastest one minute can be slower
    the next, especially during peak traffic hours.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Should I pick a provider based on this alone?</strong> No — treat
    it as one input among several (pricing, model quality, rate limits);
    latency from your own server or client will differ from this snapshot
    regardless of which provider you choose.
  </p>
</ToolLayout>
